<BaseLayout
  title="About | Ayush Prakash"
  description="Ayush Prakash studies how technology reshapes thought, agency, and society, and how people can shape the systems shaping their lives."
>
  <div style="padding-top:clamp(36px,5vw,68px)"><h1 class="dsub" style="font-size:clamp(38px,6vw,72px)">About</h1></div>
  <div class="aboutsplit">
    <div class="portrait"><img src="/img/mtl-1.jpg" alt="Ayush Prakash" /></div>
    <div class="prose">
    <p>Technology changes more than what we can do; it changes how we think, relate, and decide what matters. Ayush Prakash studies those shifts across technologies, from the systems people build to the institutions that govern them and the lives reshaped by their use. His work asks how we can meet technological change without surrendering human judgment, agency, or responsibility.</p>
    <p>He is Director of Corporate Strategy at <a href="https://newsapience.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px solid var(--hair)">New Sapience</a>, where he works on deterministic AI, non-LLM machine cognition, business operations, and institutional capital strategy.</p>
    <p>He is the author of AI for Gen Z and co-author, with neuroscientist Karl Friston, of &ldquo;The Infinity of the Brain and the Void of the Machine&rdquo; in The Montreal Review. With Dr. Ran Anbar, he wrote &ldquo;The Risks of AI and Social Media for the Developing Brain&rdquo; for Psychology Today. He also hosts the Ayush Prakash Podcast, with more than 150 conversations featuring researchers and thinkers including Friston and Avi Loeb.</p>
    <p>He lives in Montreal and works in English and French.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
